@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+# First-person domains (FP)
+
+A native-only mod for the PC port: the camera sits in the Digi-Beetle and looks along its
+facing while you explore the domains. Toggle it with **F4** or in **F1 > Display > First-person
+domains**. It is saved as `fpview = 1` in `settings.ini`, or set for one run with `--fpview`.
+
+## Controls in first person
+
+| Pad | Does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Up | Step forward |
+| Left / Right | Turn 90 degrees on the spot |
+| Down | Turn around |
+| Up + Left / Right | Step diagonally forward (the view keeps facing ahead) |
+| Cross, Square, Select ... | As before; Cross acts on the tile you face |
+
+The beetle turns back to the view after a diagonal step, so Cross always acts on what is in
+front of you.
+
+## How it works
+
+Everything is presentation. Turns, RNG, the retail draw window and the game's own state run as
+before, and the PS1 matching build is untouched: every change is inside `#ifdef DW2_NATIVE`
+(the retail-preprocessed sources of the five touched stag4000 files are identical).
+
+- **Camera** (`Stg40_FpSetCamera`, called from `Stg40_CameraUpdate`): `GsSetRefView2` from the
+  beetle's position at eye height, looking along a yaw that eases toward the facing octant. The
+  camera scripts keep running underneath (the game waits on `Stg40_CamIsMoving`).
+- **Floor** (`Stg40_FpDrawFloor`, replacing the retail 9x9 window): every cell out to
+  `STG40_FP_RADIUS` tiles, nearest first. Each tile top and wall is clipped in world space
+  against the near plane and a guard band around the screen, then projected by the GTE vertex
+  by vertex (so the PGXP hook records them and the HD renderer draws them perspective-correct),
+  and sent as `POLY_GT3` fans, faded to black with distance. The packets go into the mod's own
+  double-buffered area, a static array inside the 16 MB window the 24-bit OT links reach, so
+  the floor never takes room from the game's models. Without precise geometry (no wobble and
+  smooth frames both off) floors are cut 2 x 2 and walls 2 x 1 against affine texture warp.
+- **Walls** keep the retail templates and the retail cull rule (`Stg40_DrawTileWalls`), so
+  walled floors show walls from inside, and platform floors (cliffs going down) show the
+  cliffs that face you.
+- **Square tiles**: retail rows are 0x800 deep and columns 0xA00 wide (made for the angled
+  overhead camera). In first person both are 0xA00, and entity positions go through
+  `Stg40_FpWorldX / Z` to match. The FP world is centred on the beetle.
+- **Entities** beyond the retail 5-tile window are drawn through the 16:9 side models
+  (out to the radius). Models behind the eye are dropped by the GTE error flags per bone.
+- **The beetle** and the models linked to it are not drawn (their anims still run).
+
+## Tuning
+
+`src/stag4000/fpview_math.inc`: `FP_EYE` (eye height), `FP_H` (projection, about 80 degrees),
+`FP_PITCH` (look down), `FP_FOG_START / END`, `STG40_FP_RADIUS` (in `fpview.h`),
+`FP_TURN_STEP` (turn speed).
+
+## Known limits
+
+- No ceilings: above the walls is the black background.
+- Camera scripts (floor intro, scrolls to a target when shooting a Gift or reading enemy info)
+  are not shown; the view stays in the beetle.
+- Entities far from the beetle need their files in the CD cache, as the 16:9 side columns do.
+- Smooth frames do not blend the dungeon while in first person (30 fps there).
